# Chapters 3 and 4.

CH3 = [
    ('ch', 'METHODOLOGY'),
    ('h2', 'sec:mintro', 'Introduction'),
    ('p', "{fig:method} shows the methodology of the work. A first-generation switching node and a study of 15 degeneracy-detection rules ({sec:rules}) showed that, on Multi Floor, the error of a switching system follows the time for which control is handed to VINS-Mono rather than the quality of the detection. The fusion channels were therefore designed to weight the two sensors per direction, and their constants were fixed on recorded runs. Live runs then exposed failures that replays had not shown. Each failure was addressed by a small, testable change (versions F20 to F24), and the final version, F35, changed only the initial guess of A-LOAM. Every live test had a pass rule written before it was run, and every run is reported. A version name such as F24 denotes one fixed set of settings of the fusion node; {tab:configs} lists all versions and {sec:resintro} gives a short key."),
    ('fig', 'fig:method', '/tmp/w/v3/fc/method.png', 5.2, 'Methodology of the work'),
    ('p', "The remainder of this chapter describes the fusion node itself. {sec:overview} gives an overview of the system and of the processing of one scan, Sections {n:sec:schur} to {n:sec:chR} describe channels R and T, {sec:addT} describes the additional T channel and the heading correction, and {sec:quantities} summarises the quantities computed for each scan."),
    ('h2', 'sec:overview', 'System overview'),
    ('p', "The fusion divides the estimation by direction ({fig:arch}). For every LiDAR scan the fusion node receives the scan-to-map pose and the Hessian from A-LOAM and the pose from VINS-Mono. The rotation is taken from VINS-Mono. The translation is taken from the LiDAR along the directions that the scan constrains and from VINS-Mono along the other directions. Where VINS-Mono is unusable, that is, during its warm-up, after a loss of tracking or after a failed trust check, the LiDAR carries the whole step. The node forms the LiDAR increment (rotation vector φ<sub>L</sub>, translation Δt<sub>L</sub>) and the VINS-Mono increment (φ<sub>V</sub>, Δt<sub>V</sub>) since the previous scan and blends them with two 3 x 3 weight matrices:"),
    ('eq', 166, 'eq:fuseR'),
    ('eq', 167, 'eq:fuset'),
    ('p', "The fused pose is the previous fused pose composed with this increment. A weight of W = 0 means that VIO carries a direction, and W = I means that the LiDAR carries it. For the first 20 s after VINS-Mono starts (T<sub>warm</sub>) the output is LiDAR-only, because a monocular VIO needs time to settle its scale. The same warm-up is repeated after VINS-Mono is lost, a condition recorded as the mode VO_FAIL (no message, a jump of more than 1 m or 0.5 rad, or a restart). The loop is closed through A-LOAM: the fused pose, moved forward by a step chosen as described in {sec:statusguess}, is the initial guess of A-LOAM for the next scan. A-LOAM has no IMU of its own here, so inertial information reaches the LiDAR side only through the pose of VINS-Mono."),
    ('fig', 'fig:arch', 169, 'Architecture of the fusion node: channels R and T, the additional T channel and the scan-to-map guess'),
    ('p', "{fig:perscan} shows the same processing as a sequence of decisions for one scan. A-LOAM first matches the scan to its map. If VINS-Mono is not yet usable, the fused pose simply follows A-LOAM. Otherwise the node checks whether VINS-Mono can be trusted, computes the weights from the three channels, decides where the rotation comes from and blends the two increments. Finally, it chooses the starting point for the next scan from the degeneracy status of the last five scans. The individual steps are described in the following sections and in Sections {n:sec:safeguards} and {n:sec:statusguess}."),
    ('fig', 'fig:perscan', '/tmp/w/v3/fc/perscan.png', 4.75, 'Processing of one LiDAR scan in the fusion node'),
    ('h2', 'sec:schur', 'Rotation and translation information blocks of the Hessian'),
    ('p', "The A-LOAM Hessian is partitioned into rotation (R) and translation (t) blocks, with rotation in radians and translation in metres:"),
    ('eq', 173, 'eq:H'),
    ('p', "The Schur complements give the information about one part of the motion after the other part has been estimated from the same data:"),
    ('eq', 175, 'eq:SRR'),
    ('eq', 176, 'eq:Stt'),
    ('p', "S<sub>tt</sub> is the translation information that remains when the rotation of the scan is free, and S<sub>RR</sub> is the rotation information that remains when the translation is free. Each is a 3 x 3 matrix in a single unit, so its eigenvalues can be compared with one another. In a corridor S<sub>tt</sub> has one small eigenvalue along the axis and two large eigenvalues across it."),
    ('h2', 'sec:chT', 'Channel T: per-direction translation weighting'),
    ('p', "The translation block is converted into a weight by treating the LiDAR increment as a measurement with information c<sub>T</sub> S<sub>tt</sub> and the VIO increment as a prior with information γ I:"),
    ('eq', 180, 'eq:Wt'),
    ('p', "Along an eigenvector of S<sub>tt</sub> with eigenvalue λ the weight is c<sub>T</sub>λ / (c<sub>T</sub>λ + γ). It is close to 1 where the scene constrains the motion and close to 0 along a degenerate axis, so no switching threshold is needed; the condition-number gate of earlier versions is disabled (KAPPA_TH = 10<sup>9</sup>). The scale c<sub>T</sub> = 0.03 was chosen once on Multi Floor recordings, as the value with the smallest mean ATE in the middle of a plateau of similar values, and has not been changed since. The prior information is γ = 1 in normal operation; {sec:safeguards} describes when it is changed."),
    ('h2', 'sec:chR', 'Channel R: rotation information and rotation check'),
    ('p', "With A-LOAM, the rotation of VINS-Mono was more accurate than the LiDAR rotation on most sequences. An active rotation weight (c<sub>R</sub> > 0) improved Long Corridor in early replays, but on Multi Floor, Corridor02 and Cave02 the VIO rotation gave the lower error, and an open-loop replay of the F35 logs with c<sub>R</sub> between 10<sup>-5</sup> and 3 x 10<sup>-3</sup> raised the Cave02 error by 2.4 m at every setting ({tab:other}). In normal operation, therefore, c<sub>R</sub> = 0: the rotation comes from VIO and S<sub>RR</sub> is only monitored."),
    ('p', "A rotation check compares the VIO and LiDAR rotations over windows of 2 s and 10 s. When they disagree by more than 0.402 rad over 2 s and 0.585 rad over 10 s, the largest values observed on 19 Multi Floor recordings, the LiDAR rotation carries the step with W<sub>R</sub> = (S<sub>RR</sub> + 10<sup>-3</sup> I)<sup>-1</sup> S<sub>RR</sub>. The check closed on no fused scan of four sequences and on 3.6 % of the fused scans of GEODE, so it acts as a safeguard against strong disagreement rather than as a regular source of rotation."),
    ('h2', 'sec:addT', 'Additional T channel: orientation tensor of the scan geometry'),
    ('p', "Channels R and T are derived from the Hessian of the solver. The additional T channel examines the same question independently, from the raw points of the scan, and also supplies a heading correction."),
    ('h3', 'sec:tensor', 'Orientation tensor of the keyframe point cloud'),
    ('p', "For each LiDAR keyframe, the 12 nearest neighbours of every point are found and their covariance matrix is decomposed into eigenvalues λ<sub>1</sub> ≤ λ<sub>2</sub> ≤ λ<sub>3</sub>. A point is classified as planar when (λ<sub>2</sub> - λ<sub>1</sub>)/λ<sub>3</sub> > 0.5, with normal n, and as linear when (λ<sub>3</sub> - λ<sub>2</sub>)/λ<sub>3</sub> > 0.5, with direction d. A plane constrains translation along its normal, and an edge constrains translation in the plane perpendicular to its direction. The geometric orientation tensor accounts for both:"),
    ('eq', 187, 'eq:G'),
    ('p', "G has a trace of 1 and requires no correspondences, no solver state and no threshold. Between keyframes it is rotated into the current LiDAR frame. Planar normals alone understate the support that A-LOAM receives from edge features such as door frames and pipes, so edge directions are included; rules built on planar normals alone gave corridor errors 0.26 to 1.0 m higher in replay."),
    ('h3', 'sec:gmax', 'Bounding the translation information with the tensor'),
    ('p', "The tensor is scaled to the same total information as the solver, B = tr(H<sub>tt</sub>) G, and combined with A = S<sub>tt</sub> by a basis-free matrix rule. Several rules were compared by replay on clean data and under injected faults ({sec:faults}). The final version uses the generalised maximum:"),
    ('eq', 191, 'eq:gmax'),
    ('p', "Here max(M, I) raises every generalised eigenvalue below 1 to 1. Along each direction the combined information is therefore the larger of what the solver and the raw geometry support, and S'<sub>tt</sub> replaces S<sub>tt</sub> in Equation {n:eq:Wt}. F20 used the opposite rule, the minimum, as a fail-safe cap. The maximum was chosen in a comparison whose decision rule was written beforehand, because it scored best over the clean and fault cases and on a farm recording that the comparison had not used. Protection against a solver that is confident but wrong comes instead from the VIO-trust check of {sec:safeguards}."),
    ('h3', 'sec:sah', 'Structure-anchored heading (SAH)'),
    ('p', "The second use of the normals is a heading correction. VINS-Mono cannot observe its heading, and the heading error dominates the position error in long corridors. The near-vertical planar normals of a keyframe are levelled with the gravity direction from VIO, and their horizontal directions form an axial histogram h(θ) with 0.5° bins over 180°. The heading error is the shift, searched over ±15°, that best aligns h(θ) with a reference histogram learned in the world frame. It passes through a Kalman update and is applied at no more than 0.25° per scan. The correction switches itself off when the structure is not distinct, using a null-model test against random directions, a test for a unique correlation peak, a chi-square gate and a drift gate. This structure-anchored heading (SAH) depends on the even-order orientation tensors of the same normals. The fourth-order tensor matters because, with two equally strong perpendicular families of walls, the second-order tensor is isotropic and carries no heading information."),
    ('h2', 'sec:quantities', 'Quantities computed for each scan'),
    ('p', "{tab:quantities} summarises the quantities that the fusion node computes for each scan and explains how their values are interpreted. The same quantities appear in the logs and in the results of Chapter 5, for example in {fig:channels}."),
    ('tab', 'tab:quantities', {'widths': [1700, 3200, 3383],
                               'header': ['Quantity', 'Meaning', 'Interpretation of the value'],
                               'rows': [['H (6 x 6)', 'Registration Hessian from A-LOAM', 'A large eigenvalue marks a direction well constrained by the scan; an eigenvalue near zero marks a degenerate direction'],
                                        ['S<sub>tt</sub>, S<sub>RR</sub> (3 x 3 each)', 'Translation and rotation information after the other part has been estimated (Schur complements)', 'Compared within one unit; in a corridor S<sub>tt</sub> has one small eigenvalue along the axis'],
                                        ['G', 'Orientation tensor of the planar normals and edge directions of the raw scan', 'Trace of 1; a direction with a near-zero eigenvalue has no supporting structure'],
                                        ["S'<sub>tt</sub>", 'Translation information after the generalised maximum with the tensor', "In each direction, the larger of the solver and geometry support; a ratio λ'/λ near 1 means that both agree"],
                                        ['W<sub>t</sub> (eigenvalues 0 to 1)', 'Share of the translation taken from the LiDAR in each direction', '1: the LiDAR carries the direction; 0: VINS-Mono carries it; 0.69 means that about 31 % comes from VINS-Mono'],
                                        ['W<sub>R</sub>', 'Share of the rotation taken from the LiDAR', '0 in normal operation; set from S<sub>RR</sub> only when the rotation check closes'],
                                        ['γ', 'Information assigned to the VIO increment', '1 when VINS-Mono is trusted; 0 when the trust check fails and the LiDAR carries the step'],
                                        ['r', 'Root-mean-square log ratio of the VINS-Mono and LiDAR path lengths over 10 s', 'r ≤ 0.157: VINS-Mono is trusted; above this value it is distrusted'],
                                        ['SAH correction (°)', 'Heading correction from the directions of the walls', 'Applied at no more than 0.25° per scan; held constant when the structure is not distinct'],
                                        ['Status queue', 'Degeneracy flags of the last five scans from the published thresholds', 'Decides whether the next initial guess uses the A-LOAM step, a blend or the VINS-Mono step']]},
     'Quantities computed for each scan and their interpretation'),
]

CH4 = [
    ('ch', 'TEST SETUP'),
    ('h2', 'sec:tintro', 'Introduction'),
    ('p', "This chapter describes how the method was implemented and tested. {sec:computing} describes the computing environment and {sec:data} the datasets. Sections {n:sec:safeguards} and {n:sec:statusguess} describe the safeguards added after the first live tests and the rule that chooses the initial guess of A-LOAM in the final version. {sec:configs} lists the tested configurations, {sec:metrics} defines the evaluation metrics and the test protocol, {sec:farmsim} describes the Gazebo farm simulation, {sec:replay} the replay rates and the acceptance of runs, and {sec:baselines} the baselines."),
    ('h2', 'sec:computing', 'Computing and software setup'),
    ('p', "All live runs were made on one laptop with a Ryzen 5 5600H CPU, an Nvidia GTX 1650 GPU with 4 GB of video memory and 16 GB of RAM, running Ubuntu with Docker. ROS Noetic runs in separate containers on a Docker network, with the ROS master in the A-LOAM container ({fig:setup}). A-LOAM and the fusion node share one container, VINS-Mono runs in a second container, the recorded data (bags) are played back from a third, temporary container, and a fourth container with the full ROS desktop displays the run live in RViz. The ROS master is restarted before every run so that no state carries over from one run to the next. {tab:software} lists the software components."),
    ('fig', 'fig:setup', 201, 'Container layout of the experimental setup'),
    ('tab', 'tab:software', 204, 'Software components of the experimental setup'),
    ('h2', 'sec:data', 'Datasets'),
    ('p', "Five real sequences and one simulated route were used ({tab:sequences} and {fig:gt}). Four real sequences come from SubT-MRS [@subtmrs], recorded at Carnegie Mellon University and in the Laurel Caverns, and one comes from GEODE [@geode]. All five were recorded with a 16-beam Velodyne LiDAR at 10 Hz, a camera and an IMU. Together they cover a multi-floor building with stairs, two long corridors, a cave and a stairwell, which are the main types of degenerate environment discussed in Chapter 1."),
    ('tab', 'tab:sequences', 209, 'Sequences used in this work'),
    ('fig', 'fig:gt', 211, 'Ground-truth trajectories of the five real sequences (top view)'),
    ('p', "The SubT-MRS ground truth was obtained by registering the LiDAR against a survey map together with the platform odometry. It is therefore not fully independent of the sensors under test and is weakest where the LiDAR degenerates; on Corridor02 its heading jumps by several degrees at some instants, so only the position error is reliable on that sequence. The GEODE ground truth was obtained against a terrestrial laser scan. The residual time offsets between the bags and the ground truth were 0.03 s for Corridor02 and 0.04 s for Long Corridor."),
    ('h2', 'sec:safeguards', 'Fusion safeguards (F21 to F24)'),
    ('p', "The first live runs of the fusion revealed failures that replays of recorded logs had not shown. Each was addressed by one safeguard. The only new constants, those of the VIO-trust check and the LiDAR guard, were set on Multi Floor calibration replays."),
    ('p', "<b>VIO-trust check (F21).</b> For every scan, the ratio of the VINS-Mono and LiDAR path lengths is formed over 10 s windows within one VINS-Mono segment, and r is the root mean square of ln(|Δt<sub>V</sub>|/|Δt<sub>L</sub>|). While r stays at or below 0.157, and a faster 5 s version stays at or below 0.277, γ = 1 in Equation {n:eq:Wt}; otherwise γ = 0 and the LiDAR carries the step. Both reference values, and the guard constant described next, were set from six Multi Floor calibration replays. A LiDAR guard acts in the opposite direction: while VINS-Mono is trusted, a LiDAR path that runs more than 4.325 m ahead of the VINS-Mono path within 2 s marks the LiDAR step as failed."),
    ('p', "<b>Anchored hand-over (F22, F23).</b> In the live Long Corridor runs of F20 the fused heading jumped by 6 to 7° at the first fused scan. The warm-up had already integrated a fast turn with the LiDAR rotation, and VINS-Mono, which lagged by about 5°, then added its own catch-up of the same turn. At every entry into the fused mode the rotation is therefore reset to"),
    ('eq', 217, 'eq:handover'),
    ('p', "where R<sub>vL</sub> is the LiDAR orientation predicted by VINS-Mono, R<sub>A,k</sub> is the A-LOAM orientation at warm-up scan k, and the mean is a robust chordal mean over all warm-up scans of the current VINS-Mono segment. VINS-Mono then continues from a reference that is consistent with itself, so its catch-up removes the transient instead of adding to it."),
    ('p', "<b>Mapping-guess guard (F24).</b> In a live farm run of F23 the error reached 5.3 m in the first 18 s while VINS-Mono was failing. The likely cause, which a single run cannot prove, is that VINS-Mono steps of 1 to 3 m were passed into the initial guess of A-LOAM and that the odometry of A-LOAM followed them. In F24 a VINS-Mono step is accepted for the guess only when the previous mode was not VO_FAIL and the step is within the jump limits of 1 m and 0.5 rad. This rule does not change the fused output itself."),
    ('h2', 'sec:statusguess', 'Degeneracy-status initial guess for scan-to-map matching (F35)'),
    ('p', "Up to F24, the initial guess for the scan-to-map matching of A-LOAM was the last fused pose moved by the VINS-Mono step wherever that step was usable, and the last fused pose without motion otherwise. On Cave02 and GEODE the no-motion guess was used on 13.5 % and 78 % of the scans respectively, while VINS-Mono was starting or failing. Where the scan is well constrained, on the other hand, the VINS-Mono step only adds the noise of VINS-Mono to a match that does not need it."),
    ('p', "F35 chooses the guess with the status rule of Switch-SLAM [@switch]. The six eigenvalues of H are computed, and the three smallest, scaled to unit length, are compared with the published thresholds of 0.12, 0.27 and 0.48; the scan is classed as degenerate if any of them falls below its threshold. The flags of the last five scans form a status queue. With Δ<sub>L</sub> the scan-to-scan increment of A-LOAM and Δ<sub>V</sub> the increment of VINS-Mono, the step added to the last fused pose is"),
    ('eq', 223, 'eq:guess'),
    ('p', "with w = min(1, √3 λ̄<sub>1</sub>) computed from the smallest scaled eigenvalue (Equation 13 of Switch-SLAM). A VINS-Mono step that fails the F24 guard counts as absent. Only the starting point of A-LOAM changes; the output is still the fusion of Equations {n:eq:fuseR} and {n:eq:fuset}. Unlike in Switch-SLAM, the output is neither switched nor remapped. That structure, with the pose of A-LOAM as the output and the channels of this work as the detector, was tested as F30 to F34, and the weighted fusion gave the lower error on every sequence on which both were run ({tab:other})."),
    ('p', "A variant, F26, drives the same status queue with channels R, T and the additional T instead of the published thresholds. It put the VINS-Mono step into the guess on 73 % of the Long Corridor scans, against 2 % for F35. On the four sequences with runs, the decision rule written beforehand showed no gain over F35: Corridor02 was higher (2.82 m against 1.99 m) and the other comparisons were inconclusive. F35 therefore remains the final version, and {tab:f26} compares F26 and F35 on every sequence."),
    ('h2', 'sec:configs', 'Tested configurations and the final configuration'),
    ('p', "Each configuration is a set of environment variables passed to the fusion node, and {tab:configs} lists those referred to in this report. No constant was fitted to a test sequence after F20: the new constants of F21 come from Multi Floor, F22 to F24 add none, and the thresholds of F35 are the published values."),
    ('tab', 'tab:configs', 229, 'Configurations of the fusion node used in this work'),
    ('p', "<b>Choice of the final version.</b> F35 was chosen as the final version. It keeps all three channels and every safeguard of F24 and changes only the starting point of A-LOAM. Against F24 it gave a lower error on GEODE, Cave02 and the farm route, a similar error on Multi Floor and Long Corridor, and a higher error on Corridor02, each from one to four runs ({sec:guess}). Its constants are listed in {tab:constants}."),
    ('tab', 'tab:constants', 233, 'Frozen constants of the final configuration (F35)'),
    ('h2', 'sec:metrics', 'Evaluation metrics and protocol'),
    ('p', "Every result in Chapter 5 answers the same question: how far is the estimated path from the true path? The true path, or ground truth, comes from the dataset or, in the simulation, from the Gazebo model pose. At each scan time the estimator gives a position p<sub>i</sub> and the ground truth gives a position g<sub>i</sub>. The two are expressed in different coordinate frames, and the estimator starts from an arbitrary origin, so the estimated path is first fitted to the true path by a single rotation and translation ({fig:ate}). The distance that remains at each scan is the error e<sub>i</sub>. The ATE is the root mean square of these distances and represents a typical error in metres; the maximum error is the largest single distance. The formal definition follows, and {tab:howtoread} explains how each quantity in Chapter 5 is read."),
    ('fig', 'fig:ate', 237, 'Computation of the absolute trajectory error (schematic): the estimated path is fitted to the ground truth, and the remaining distances are the errors'),
    ('p', "The accuracy measure is the absolute trajectory error [@sturm]. The estimated positions p<sub>i</sub> are aligned with the ground truth g<sub>i</sub> by the rigid transformation (R, t) that minimises the squared error, without scale [@umeyama], over the full span of the run, and"),
    ('eq', 240, 'eq:ate'),
    ('p', "The maximum of ||R p<sub>i</sub> + t - g<sub>i</sub>|| is reported alongside the ATE, as in Switch-SLAM, and all results are for the front end only, without loop closure. A replay feeds the logged A-LOAM and VINS-Mono poses of a run through the real fusion node. It is exact for the fusion but open loop, because A-LOAM is not re-seeded with the fused pose. Only live runs are compared with published values, and differences below max(0.1 m, 10 %) are treated as ties."),
    ('p', "The statistics follow Ganesan et al. [@ganesan]: the minimum, median, maximum and mean for each environment; a success rate, here the share of runs with an ATE of 10 m or less; and a one-sided test of H<sub>0</sub>: M(baseline) = M(proposed) against H<sub>1</sub>: M(baseline) > M(proposed) at the 95 % level. The test used here is the exact Mann-Whitney U test on separate live runs, with the Welch t-test reported alongside where both groups have two or more runs. With n<sub>1</sub> and n<sub>2</sub> runs, the exact test cannot give a p-value below 1 / C(n<sub>1</sub> + n<sub>2</sub>, n<sub>2</sub>). Cave02 and GEODE were initially held out, but the F24 results on them led to F25 to F27, so all six sequences are development data for F35."),
    ('tab', 'tab:howtoread', 244, 'Interpretation of the quantities reported in Chapter 5'),
    ('h2', 'sec:farmsim', 'Gazebo farm simulation'),
    ('p', "The farm test uses the Clearpath agriculture world in Gazebo with a Husky robot carrying a VLP-16 LiDAR (10 Hz), an RGB camera (640 x 480, 60 Hz) and an IMU (200 Hz), which is the sensor set given for the simulated robot of Switch-SLAM ({fig:gazebo}). A waypoint driver moves the robot along an L-shaped route from (0, 0) to (0, -40) and then to (-40, -40) at 0.25 m/s, turning in place at the start and at the corner. The ground truth is the Gazebo model pose. Gazebo, A-LOAM, VINS-Mono (initialised by a short oscillating motion) and the fusion node are started first; the robot is then reset to the origin, and the driver starts once the node is in the fused mode."),
    ('fig', 'fig:gazebo', 248, 'Husky robot model used in the Gazebo agriculture world'),
    ('h2', 'sec:replay', 'Replay rate and acceptance of runs'),
    ('p', "A replay can lose scans if the computer cannot process them in real time. The replay rate was therefore lowered where needed. The rate was chosen from the row counts alone, before any error was computed, and runs were accepted only if they contained enough scans ({tab:replay}). The fusion node writes one log row per scan, containing the fused, A-LOAM and VINS-Mono poses, the weights, the Hessian, the tensor and heading values, the guess type and the mode, and it stores the keyframe scans for replays and map renders. Failed starts and diverged runs are reported, not discarded."),
    ('tab', 'tab:replay', 253, 'Replay rates and number of scans logged per run'),
    ('h2', 'sec:baselines', 'Baselines'),
    ('p', "The baselines are VINS-Mono alone (F0 runs; on GEODE and the farm route, the VINS-Mono output logged during the fused runs, which is valid because VINS-Mono takes no input from the fusion), A-LOAM alone (F5), F8 (channel T only) and F24. The published front-end results of Switch-SLAM [@switch], ATE RMSE / maximum error of 1.61 / 3.63 m on Multi Floor, 2.42 / 5.09 m on Long Corridor and 0.38 / 1.10 m on the farm route, are the external targets. An implementation of Switch-SLAM within this software stack did not reproduce them ({tab:other})."),
    ('p', "Three of the ten datasets of Switch-SLAM are compared here: Multi Floor, Long Corridor and the farm. The comparison is not like for like. Switch-SLAM matches each scan to its map with a LiDAR-inertial front end, starts the match from a guess propagated with the IMU, and switches between the LiDAR and visual estimates. A-LOAM has no IMU, and this work blends the two estimates direction by direction. The published values are means of three runs, whereas the values here come from one to four runs, cover the front end only and are scored with the protocol of {sec:metrics}."),
]
